Trace McSorley could have been a safety in college.
The Penn State redshirt sophomore and starting quarterback has a well-told story by now, the one that most fans are familiar with: multi-time state title winner in high school, talented quarterback, winner in every sense of the word.
But what many forget, or may not know, is that when he was first recruited by the then-Vanderbilt staff that would ultimately end up at Penn State, it was very possible that the two-way Briar Woods' star's future, if he became a Commodore, would lead to him playing defense.
"I watched his tape; I liked it as a QB," Penn State tight ends coach Ricky Rahne said on Thursday. He was then Vanderbilt's quarterback coach, a role he also held at PSU until this season.
"I wasn't quite sure, but I loved him as an athlete and as a safety and I gave him to our defensive coaches and they liked him, and they offered him as a safety."
Yet Rahne, who has coached mostly quarterbacks and tight ends during his career and was Vandy's QB coach at the time, just couldn't forget what he saw from McSorley as a passer, so he took advantage of spring evaluation period travel time to visit Ashburn, Va., to watch him throw.
"The thing that sold me was his accuracy," Rahne said. "He has a very strong arm, but No. 1 was his accuracy."
The script flipped from there, and McSorley eventually committed to play in the SEC as a QB. Then, when James Franklin, Rahne, and others left to take over the Lions, McSorley followed, and the rest is history.
"At safety, he had good speed, he had quickness, he was always around the ball, had great instincts, and the other thing, he was tough," Rahne said. "That toughness, you can see week in and week out."
Bowers update
Penn State lost tight end Nick Bowers for the season before he could show how much he improved during a redshirt year in 2015, but what he's lost in playing time has not kept him from improving, Rahne said.
"Nick is an extremely well-liked guy on the team," he said. "He's still in our meetings, which is a great thing, because our guys love him. [He's] making sure he's preparing as if he's going to play football{g} again{g}."{g}
