It is more than 30 years since a new museum opened in Paris. The last time was in 1977 when the Palais Galliera became the Museum of Fashion and Costume. But now the private Pinacothèque has become the city's latest museum.
The building on Place de la Madeleine is a familiar sight. It made a name for itself with a successful series of temporary exhibitions, ranging from the Xi'an Terracotta Warriors in 2008 to The Incas' Gold, which has just closed. Last month a new branch opened, extending over 3,000 square metres, just round the corner at 8 Rue Vignon. Its head Marc Restellini has generated plenty of publicity to launch the new venue with Birth of a Museum, a three-part show built on a core permanent collection and two temporary exhibitions. The first focuses on the Romanov tsars who initiated the Hermitage museum in St Petersburg. The second looks at the Esterházys, the great Austro-Hungarian family whose collections form the backbone of Budapest's Museum of Fine Arts.
The Pinacothèque's own collection, formed of long-term loans from private collectors, is sandwiched between the two. When the other two shows close at the end of May, the collection will spread over the two floors of the new building. Temporary exhibitions will continue in the old building.
Staging three exhibitions in one go is quite an achievement. "It seemed appropriate to open the new museum with a tribute to collectors and the museums to which they gave rise," Restellini says. "The last Esterházy, Nicholas II, was crazy about art. He bought everything he could. He is the first collector whose name is associated with a painting, Raphael's Esterházy Madonna."
Restellini's critics, and there are many, condemn his extravagance in bringing two shows in, but he is unmoved: "I am paying for the research work carried out on our account by the museums we are hosting, but I am certainly not renting their works." Mikhail Piotrovsky, who heads the Hermitage, endorses this point. He is delighted at the partnership: "We are the authors of this exhibition, which aims to show how important Europe was for imperial Russia. All too often people want to borrow works for projects in which we have no say. Here the Hermitage can show itself in its true light."
